                    <h3 id="bridge" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="sys-dot" style={`--sys: ${systems.otel.color}`}></span>The bridge sink</h3>
                    <pre><code>{otelInstall}</code></pre>
                    <p>
                        Maple's <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-python">Python instrumentation guide</a> sets up OpenTelemetry in a <code>telemetry.py</code> file, including a <code>logger_provider</code> that sends logs to Maple. Add the bridge to the end of that file:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(bridgeCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        The guide's <code>telemetry.py</code> ends with a line that attaches a <code>LoggingHandler</code> to the root logger. If you use <code>intercept.py</code>, delete that line, or every library log is sent twice.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="in-a-request" class="scroll-mt-24">Log inside a request</h3>
                    <p>
                        A <strong>span</strong> is one timed step of a trace. Web framework instrumentation starts a span for each request, and every line logged while it runs carries its trace ID. Here the span is started by hand:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(requestCode, "python-logging")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>order_id</code> arrives as a log attribute next to the file, function and line, and the record carries the trace ID of the <code>POST /checkout</code> span.
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-callout">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Two ways to break the bridge</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Adding <code>otel_sink</code> with <code>enqueue=True</code>. The queue hands the line to another thread with no active span, so the trace ID arrives as all zeros.</li>
                            <li>A <code>bind()</code> key that <code>logging</code> already uses, such as <code>name</code>, <code>module</code> or <code>message</code>. <code>logging</code> rejects it and the line never reaches OpenTelemetry. Use names like <code>user_name</code>.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <h3 id="maple" class="scroll-mt-24">Loguru logs in Maple</h3>
                    <ol class="guide-steps">
                        <li>
                            <span>01</span>
                            <div><h4>Point telemetry.py at Maple</h4><p>Set <code>MAPLE_ENDPOINT</code> and your ingest key as the <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-python">Python guide</a> describes. Lines from the bridge arrive like any other OpenTelemetry log.</p></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>02</span>
                            <div><h4>Find the lines</h4><p>Open <strong>Logs</strong> and filter by <strong>Service</strong> or <strong>Severity</strong> in the sidebar. Paste a trace ID into <strong>Search</strong> to list every line from one request.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/loguru-01-logs.webp" data-zoomable alt="The Logs page filtered to the checkout service and one trace ID, listing the four lines one POST /checkout request wrote through Loguru: two INFO, one WARN and one SUCCESS." width="2362" height="1105" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>03</span>
                            <div><h4>Open a line</h4><p>Click a row, then <strong>Open detail</strong>. Bound values such as <code>order_id</code> are under <strong>Log Attributes</strong>, and the <code>trace:</code> link opens the request the line belongs to.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/loguru-02-log-detail.webp" data-zoomable alt="The log detail sheet for Charging card: the trace link and span ID in the meta strip, and Log Attributes code.file.path, code.function.name, code.line.number and order_id." width="1342" height="866" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                    </ol>

                    <div class="guide-maple">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Set it up in Maple</p>
                        <h3>Send your first Loguru line</h3>
                        <p>The Python guide has the full <code>telemetry.py</code>, the environment-variable setup and troubleshooting.</p>
                        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
